# Performance and UX Standards

## Core Principle

Performance is not an afterthought. Every feature must feel instant to the user, never sluggish or "facepalmy". The following rules are mandatory unless explicitly waived for a specific case with documented reasoning.

## A. Database Layer (PostgreSQL + Drizzle)

### A1. Cursor-based pagination, never offset

For any list that may grow beyond ~100 rows, use cursor-based pagination instead of LIMIT/OFFSET. Offset forces Postgres to scan and discard skipped rows — degrades linearly with offset size.

Pattern:

```typescript
// API endpoint
GET /api/claims?cursor=<last-seen-id>&limit=50
// Returns: { items: [...], nextCursor: "uuid" | null }

// Drizzle query
const query = db
  .select()
  .from(claims)
  .orderBy(desc(claims.createdAt))
  .limit(pageSize + 1)
if (cursor) query.where(lt(claims.createdAt, cursorDate))
```

### A2. Composite + partial indexes from day 1

Every new table that will be queried with WHERE clauses on multiple columns needs composite indexes. Order columns by selectivity (most selective first).

Use partial indexes for hot status filters (e.g., WHERE status = 'open'):

```typescript
// Up to 275x performance improvement vs full index
index('claims_active_by_user')
  .on(claims.assignedTo, claims.createdAt.desc())
  .where(sql`${claims.status} IN ('open', 'in_progress')`)
```

### A3. Selective columns, never `SELECT *`

Lists: only columns shown in the UI. Detail views: full row.

```typescript
// List endpoint — minimal columns
db.select({
  id: claims.id,
  claimNumber: claims.claimNumber,
  status: claims.status,
  createdAt: claims.createdAt,
}).from(claims)

// Detail endpoint — full row + relations
db.query.claims.findFirst({
  where: eq(claims.id, id),
  with: { customer: true, files: true, findings: true },
})
```

### A4. Prepared statements for hot paths

Queries that run on every request (session lookup, user role check) should use prepared statements:

```typescript
const getSessionByToken = db
  .select()
  .from(sessions)
  .where(eq(sessions.token, sql.placeholder('token')))
  .prepare('get_session_by_token')
```

### A5. Connection pooling (already configured in @mr/db)

- max: 20 connections
- idleTimeoutMillis: 30000
- connectionTimeoutMillis: 2000

### A6. JOINs, never N+1 queries

Use Drizzle relations API (`with`) for related data. Never loop over results and query inside the loop.

### A7. Indexes on FK + WHERE columns mandatory

Every foreign key gets an index. Every column appearing in WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY across the codebase gets an index. Schema PRs without indexes are rejected.

## B. API Layer (Hono + Better-Auth)

### B1. Structured logging with duration tracking

Already configured via Pino. Every endpoint logs {method, path, status, durationMs}. Alert threshold: p95 > 200ms.

### B2. Cursor-based response shape

Standard list response:

```typescript
{
  items: T[],
  nextCursor: string | null,
  hasMore: boolean
}
```

### B3. Rate limiting on critical endpoints

- Login: max 5 attempts/min/IP
- Read endpoints: 100 req/sec/user
- Write endpoints: 30 req/sec/user
- File uploads: 10/min/user

Use @hono/rate-limiter when added in Phase 1.0.

## C. Frontend Data Layer (TanStack Query + Router)

### C1. queryOptions factory pattern

Define reusable query options once, use everywhere with type safety:

```typescript
// packages/shared/src/queries/claims.ts
export function claimDetailOptions(id: string) {
  return queryOptions({
    queryKey: ['claims', id],
    queryFn: () => fetch(`/api/claims/${id}`).then((r) => r.json()),
    staleTime: 30_000,
  })
}

// Component
const { data } = useSuspenseQuery(claimDetailOptions(id))
```

### C2. Route loaders for prefetching

Every route with data dependencies must use a loader to prefetch:

```typescript
const Route = createFileRoute('/claims/$id')({
  loader: ({ context, params }) =>
    context.queryClient.ensureQueryData(claimDetailOptions(params.id)),
  component: ClaimDetail,
})
```

This enables "render-as-you-fetch" — data starts loading before the component mounts.

### C3. useSuspenseQuery + React Suspense over isLoading checks

Avoid:

```typescript
if (isLoading) return <Spinner /> // ❌
```

Prefer:

```typescript
// Component assumes data exists
const { data } = useSuspenseQuery(options)

// Parent has Suspense boundary
<Suspense fallback={<SkeletonList />}>
  <ClaimsList />
</Suspense>
```

### C4. Optimistic updates for every mutation

Every mutation that updates UI state must implement optimistic updates with rollback:

```typescript
useMutation({
  mutationFn: updateClaim,
  onMutate: async (newClaim) => {
    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['claims'] })
    const previous = queryClient.getQueryData(['claims'])
    queryClient.setQueryData(['claims'], (old) =>
      old.map((c) => (c.id === newClaim.id ? newClaim : c)),
    )
    return { previous }
  },
  onError: (err, _, context) => {
    queryClient.setQueryData(['claims'], context.previous)
    toast.error('Greška pri ažuriranju')
  },
  onSettled: () => {
    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['claims'] })
  },
})
```

### C5. staleTime + gcTime tuning per query type

- **List queries:** staleTime: 30s, gcTime: 5min
- **Detail queries:** staleTime: 1min, gcTime: 5min
- **Reference data (departments, statuses):** staleTime: Infinity, gcTime: Infinity
- **Real-time data (notifications):** staleTime: 0

### C6. Request deduplication + cancellation

Native to TanStack Query — no manual work needed. Use AbortSignal in queryFn for fetch cancellation on unmount.

## D. Frontend UI Layer

### D1. Lazy-loaded routes

TanStack Router code-splits routes by default. No manual work, but verify per-route bundle sizes stay reasonable (< 50KB gzipped per route).

### D2. Skeleton loaders, never spinners

Use the Skeleton component from @mr/ui. Skeleton must visually match the loading content's structure (not generic boxes).

### D3. TanStack Virtual for lists > 100 items

Add @tanstack/react-virtual when implementing claims list, findings list, files list. Render only visible rows.

### D4. Debounced inputs

Search, filter, and validation inputs use 300ms debounce. Use the useDebouncedValue hook from @mr/shared (create if missing).

### D5. Image optimization on upload

Client-side compression before upload using browser-image-compression:

- Max width: 1920px
- Max quality: 0.85
- Convert HEIC to JPEG

### D6. Form submission UX standards

- Submit button: disabled + inline spinner during pending state
- Form fields: disabled during pending state
- Form does NOT reset until success confirmed
- Toast notification on success/error
- Error states inline on fields (not just toast)

### D7. Animation timing standards

- Microinteractions (hover, click feedback): 150-200ms
- Component transitions (modal, drawer): 200-300ms
- Page transitions: 200ms max
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion media query

## E. Bundle Size

### E1. Tree-shaking discipline

```typescript
// ❌ Pulls entire lodash
import _ from 'lodash'

// ✅ Pulls only debounce
import debounce from 'lodash/debounce'
```

### E2. Bundle size budgets per app

- admin-web: < 250KB gzipped initial
- internal-web: < 250KB gzipped initial
- portal-web: < 150KB gzipped initial (simpler app)

CI will fail builds that exceed budgets (add bundlewatch in Phase 1.0 or 1.1).

### E3. Font loading

Figtree Variable: font-display: swap (already configured). Preload font in <head> for critical text.

## F. Performance Budgets (Targets)

| Metric                    | Target |
| ------------------------- | ------ |
| Initial page load (3G)    | < 2s   |
| Time to Interactive       | < 3s   |
| API endpoint p95          | < 200ms |
| Database query p95        | < 50ms |
| First Contentful Paint    | < 1.5s |
| Largest Contentful Paint  | < 2.5s |

Measure with Lighthouse CI (add when staging is up — Tačka 12).

## G. Future-Proofing (Prepare, Don't Implement Yet)

### G1. Zustand standby

When any React Context grows to > 5 values OR > 3 listeners, refactor to Zustand. Watch for this during Phase 1.2 (Dashboard) and Phase 1.5 (Statistics) — they will likely trigger the rule.

Pattern when you migrate:

```typescript
// Before: Context with many values causes unnecessary re-renders
const FilterContext = createContext({ status, dateRange, ... })

// After: Zustand store with selective subscriptions
const useFilterStore = create((set) => ({ ... }))
const status = useFilterStore((s) => s.status) // only re-renders on status change
```

### G2. CDN-ready file API

When implementing file uploads (Phase 1.3), the API must return public URLs that CAN be rerouted through a CDN later. Do NOT serve files from the application server. Use S3-compatible storage (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2) from day one with signed URLs for private files.

This means Phase 1.3 file API design must include:

- POST /api/files/sign-upload → returns presigned URL for direct client→S3 upload
- File records in DB store the S3 key, not the URL
- Public/private file access controlled by signed URLs with expiry

### G3. PWA stand-by

Vite PWA plugin can be added in Phase 2 if mobile use case becomes priority. Current stack is PWA-ready (Vite + service worker support). Do not enable yet.

## H. Explicitly NOT Implementing (and Why)

The following are intentionally out of scope. Adding any of these requires explicit architectural review.

- **React Server Components (RSC)** — TanStack Start doesn't support yet. Would require migrating to Next.js. Not justified by our use case (highly interactive internal app).
- **Edge runtime (Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Edge)** — All users in Serbia. Frankfurt region Railway has ~20-30ms latency. Edge benefit minimal, complexity high.
- **GraphQL** — REST + Hono + Zod schemas + TanStack Query cover our needs. Migration possible later if 15+ heterogeneous clients emerge.
- **Apollo / urql** — Only relevant if GraphQL adopted.
- **Service Workers** — Internal app, users have stable office internet. Revisit if mobile-first phase emerges.

If any of these become tempting, write the architectural justification first. Default: stay with current stack.
